(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("mlops",{"mlops/what-mlops-is-and-how-ml-systems-fail":{"html":"\u003cp>A normal service fails loudly: a crash, an error rate, a timeout. A machine learning system can fail while every dashboard is green. The server answers, the latency is fine, and the predictions are slowly getting worse. MLOps is the set of habits that make those quiet failures visible and fixable, by applying the same ideas you already use for software: versioning, testing, automated delivery and monitoring.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Name the parts of a production ML system beyond the model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain why a model can be wrong while the service looks healthy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Define training-serving skew and silent failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Say what MLOps adds to DevOps and what it does not replace\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-model-is-the-small-box\">The model is the small box\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Code for the model itself is usually a small part of the system. Around it sit data collection, data checks, feature computation, training, evaluation, packaging, serving, monitoring and the process to retrain. Most production trouble lives in those surrounding parts. If you only version the model file, you have versioned the smallest piece.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-things-change-not-one\">Two things change, not one\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In ordinary software the code changes and the behaviour follows. In ML the code, the data and the model all change, and the behaviour depends on all three. The same code with new data gives a different model. A model that never changed can degrade because the world changed under it. So you must track data and model versions as carefully as code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"training-serving-skew\">Training-serving skew\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Skew means the model sees different inputs in production than it saw in training. The cause is often boring: a feature computed in a notebook with one library and in the service with another, a unit that changed, a column that is now empty, or a time zone. The model is not broken. It is being fed something it never learned from.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"silent-failure\">Silent failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A fraud model that starts approving more risky payments raises no error. A recommendation model that favours old items still returns a list. You only see the damage through a business metric or a quality check, often days later. This is why monitoring in MLOps includes the data and the predictions, not only CPU and latency.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-mlops-adds\">What MLOps adds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add four things to your DevOps toolbox: data and model versioning, automated evaluation as a release gate, monitoring of inputs and outputs, and a retraining path that is as safe as a code deploy. You still need containers, pipelines, observability and on-call.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-healthy-service-can-hide\">What a healthy service can hide\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s localhost:8080/health\ncurl -s localhost:8080/metrics | grep -E &quot;latency|errors&quot;\npython check_inputs.py --window 24h\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:8080/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;status&quot;:&quot;ok&quot;,&quot;model&quot;:&quot;churn-v14&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s localhost:8080/metrics | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;latency|errors&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">predict_latency_seconds_p95 0.041\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">predict_errors_total 0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python check_inputs.py --window 24h\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature days_since_login: 31% null (training: 0.2% null)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature plan_tier: 4 new values not seen in training\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The service is healthy by every operational signal, yet a third of one feature is missing. Only a data check shows the problem. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A feature pipeline changes upstream and fills a column with nulls. A model trained on last year stays in production for two years. Training used a clean sample while production sees messy data. Nobody owns the question &quot;is the model still good?&quot;, so nobody asks it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How What MLOps is and how ML systems fail fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The model is the small box\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Two things change, not one\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Training-serving skew\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Silent failure\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What MLOps adds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What a healthy service can hide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The model is the small box to Two things change, not one; 2, Two things change, not one to Training-serving skew; 3, Training-serving skew to Silent failure; 4, Silent failure to What MLOps adds; 5, What MLOps adds to What a healthy service can hide.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The model is the small box \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Code for the model itself is usually a small part of the system.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Versioning the model file but not the data or features.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Two things change, not one \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> In ordinary software the code changes and the behaviour follows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Treating a deployed model as finished.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Training-serving skew \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Skew means the model sees different inputs in production than it saw in training.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Computing features twice, once for training and once for serving.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Silent failure \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A fraud model that starts approving more risky payments raises no error.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Monitoring only latency and errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>What MLOps adds \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add four things to your DevOps toolbox: data and model versioning, automated evaluation as a release gate, monitoring of inputs and outputs, and a retraining path that...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>What a healthy service can hide \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The service is healthy by every operational signal, yet a third of one feature is missing.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-green-dashboard-and-a-falling-conversion-rate\">Incident: A green dashboard and a falling conversion rate\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team shipped a ranking model and watched latency and errors. Both stayed healthy. Weeks later the business noticed a drop in conversions. An upstream change had renamed a field, so one important feature arrived empty and the model quietly ranked worse.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python check_inputs.py --window 7d\ngit log --oneline -1 upstream-schema\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python check_inputs.py --window 7d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature item_popularity: 100% null since 2026-03-02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature price_bucket: ok\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -1 upstream-schema\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. The lesson is real and common: input checks on every feature, with an alert when a feature goes null or shifts, would have caught this on day one instead of week three.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Our model has 99.9 percent uptime but results got worse. What do you check?&quot; Look at input data first (nulls, ranges, new categories), then compare live feature distributions with training, then check upstream changes, then evaluate the model on recent labelled data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Monitoring only latency and errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning the model file but not the data or features\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Computing features twice, once for training and once for serving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a deployed model as finished\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-model-is-the-small-box","text":"The model is the small box"},{"id":"two-things-change-not-one","text":"Two things change, not one"},{"id":"training-serving-skew","text":"Training-serving skew"},{"id":"silent-failure","text":"Silent failure"},{"id":"what-mlops-adds","text":"What MLOps adds"},{"id":"what-a-healthy-service-can-hide","text":"What a healthy service can hide"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-a-green-dashboard-and-a-falling-conversion-rate","text":"Incident: A green dashboard and a falling conversion rate"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google: Rules of Machine Learning","u":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/rules-of-ml"},{"t":"Google Cloud: MLOps pipelines","u":"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning"}],"text":"A normal service fails loudly: a crash, an error rate, a timeout. A machine learning system can fail while every dashboard is green. The server answers, the latency is fine, and the predictions are slowly getting worse. MLOps is the set of habits that make those quiet failures visible and fixable, by applying the same ideas you already use for software: versioning, testing, automated delivery and monitoring. What you will learn Name the parts of a production ML system beyond the model Explain why a model can be wrong while the service looks healthy Define training-serving skew and silent failure Say what MLOps adds to DevOps and what it does not replace The model is the small box Code for the model itself is usually a small part of the system. Around it sit data collection, data checks, feature computation, training, evaluation, packaging, serving, monitoring and the process to retrain. Most production trouble lives in those surrounding parts. If you only version the model file, you have versioned the smallest piece. Two things change, not one In ordinary software the code changes and the behaviour follows. In ML the code, the data and the model all change, and the behaviour depends on all three. The same code with new data gives a different model. A model that never changed can degrade because the world changed under it. So you must track data and model versions as carefully as code. Training-serving skew Skew means the model sees different inputs in production than it saw in training. The cause is often boring: a feature computed in a notebook with one library and in the service with another, a unit that changed, a column that is now empty, or a time zone. The model is not broken. It is being fed something it never learned from. Silent failure A fraud model that starts approving more risky payments raises no error. A recommendation model that favours old items still returns a list. You only see the damage through a business metric or a quality check, often days later. This is why monitoring in MLOps includes the data and the predictions, not only CPU and latency. What MLOps adds Add four things to your DevOps toolbox: data and model versioning, automated evaluation as a release gate, monitoring of inputs and outputs, and a retraining path that is as safe as a code deploy. You still need containers, pipelines, observability and on-call. What a healthy service can hide $ curl -s localhost:8080/health {\"status\":\"ok\",\"model\":\"churn-v14\"} $ curl -s localhost:8080/metrics | grep -E \"latency|errors\" predict_latency_seconds_p95 0.041 predict_errors_total 0 $ python check_inputs.py --window 24h feature days_since_login: 31% null (training: 0.2% null) feature plan_tier: 4 new values not seen in training The service is healthy by every operational signal, yet a third of one feature is missing. Only a data check shows the problem. The output is a simulated illustration. How it fails A feature pipeline changes upstream and fills a column with nulls. A model trained on last year stays in production for two years. Training used a clean sample while production sees messy data. Nobody owns the question \"is the model still good?\", so nobody asks it. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How What MLOps is and how ML systems fail fits together Flow in order: 1, The model is the small box to Two things change, not one; 2, Two things change, not one to Training-serving skew; 3, Training-serving skew to Silent failure; 4, Silent failure to What MLOps adds; 5, What MLOps adds to What a healthy service can hide. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The model is the small box Step What it does: Code for the model itself is usually a small part of the system. If it fails or is weak: Versioning the model file but not t"},"mlops/data-and-feature-pipelines-for-ml":{"html":"\u003cp>Most machine learning incidents start in the data, not in the model. A feature pipeline is a production system like any other, with inputs that change, jobs that fail and schemas that drift. This lesson covers how to check data before it is used, how to version it so a result can be reproduced, and how to compute features once so training and serving agree.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Validate data against a schema and expectations before training\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Version datasets so a model can be traced to its data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain what a feature store is for and when you do not need one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep training and serving features computed by the same code\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"treat-data-like-code-and-like-an-api\">Treat data like code and like an API\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Data has a shape: columns, types, ranges and meanings. Write that shape down as a schema and test every batch against it. A column that is suddenly 40 percent null, a value outside a plausible range, or a new category should stop the pipeline or raise an alert, just as a failing unit test stops a build.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"useful-checks\">Useful checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with a short list: row count within an expected band, no unexpected nulls, values within range, categories from a known set, no duplicate keys, freshness (the newest record is recent), and a distribution check against a reference. These catch most real problems and run in seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"version-the-data\">Version the data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you cannot say which data trained a model, you cannot reproduce it or explain a bad result. Store datasets as immutable snapshots with an id, or use a tool that records content hashes next to your code commit. Never overwrite a training set in place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"point-in-time-correctness\">Point-in-time correctness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When you build training rows, each feature must use only information that existed at the time of the label. Using today&#39;s value to predict yesterday&#39;s outcome leaks the future and makes the model look great in testing and fail in production. Join features as of the event time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-definition-of-a-feature\">One definition of a feature\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write each feature once and use the same code for the training set and for serving. A feature store is a managed way to do that: it keeps an offline copy for training and an online copy for low-latency lookups, with one definition. Small teams can start with a shared library and add a store later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-data-check-you-can-run-in-a-pipeline\">A data check you can run in a pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# checks.yaml: expectations for the daily training table\ntable: churn_training\nchecks:\n  - row_count: {min: 90000, max: 130000}\n  - freshness: {column: event_date, max_age_hours: 30}\n  - not_null: [customer_id, plan_tier, event_date]\n  - unique: [customer_id, event_date]\n  - range: {column: days_since_login, min: 0, max: 3650}\n  - allowed_values: {column: plan_tier, values: [free, basic, pro, team]}\n  - null_rate: {column: last_payment_amount, max: 0.05}\non_fail: stop_pipeline_and_page\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># checks.yaml: expectations for the daily training table\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">table:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">churn_training\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">checks:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">row_count:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">min:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90000\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">130000\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">freshness:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">column:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">event_date\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max_age_hours:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">not_null:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">customer_id\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">plan_tier\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">event_date\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unique:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">customer_id\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">event_date\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">range:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">column:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">days_since_login\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">min:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3650\u003c/span>}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowed_values:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">column:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">plan_tier\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">values:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">free\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">basic\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pro\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team\u003c/span>]}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">null_rate:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">column:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">last_payment_amount\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.05\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">on_fail:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">stop_pipeline_and_page\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each line is a test on data, evaluated before training starts. A failing check stops the pipeline instead of training on bad rows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A schema changes upstream and the pipeline keeps running. A join silently drops half the rows. A feature uses information from after the event. The training set is edited by hand and nobody recorded how. A fix is applied to serving but not to training.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Data and feature pipelines for ML fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Treat data like code and like an API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Useful checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Version the data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Point-in-time correctness\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">One definition of a feature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A data check you can run in a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Treat data like code and like an API to Useful checks; 2, Useful checks to Version the data; 3, Version the data to Point-in-time correctness; 4, Point-in-time correctness to One definition of a feature; 5, One definition of a feature to A data check you can run in a.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Treat data like code and like an API \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Data has a shape: columns, types, ranges and meanings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Training on data that was never validated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Useful checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start with a short list: row count within an expected band, no unexpected nulls, values within range, categories from a known set, no duplicate keys, freshness (the...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Version the data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If you cannot say which data trained a model, you cannot reproduce it or explain a bad result.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overwriting a dataset in place.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Point-in-time correctness \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> When you build training rows, each feature must use only information that existed at the time of the label.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using future information in a training feature.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>One definition of a feature \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Write each feature once and use the same code for the training set and for serving.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Writing features twice, once for training and once for serving.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A data check you can run in a \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each line is a test on data, evaluated before training starts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-feature-that-knew-the-future\">Incident: The feature that knew the future\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team built a model to predict customer cancellations and reported excellent test results. In production it performed far worse. One feature, &quot;support tickets this month&quot;, included tickets raised after the customer had already decided to leave, which the model could not know at prediction time.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python audit_features.py --model churn-v9\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python audit_features.py --model churn-v9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature support_tickets_month: uses data up to month end\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">label cancel_date: can precede month end\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">leakage risk: HIGH\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration of a common mistake called leakage. Building training rows with point-in-time joins, so a feature only uses data from before the prediction moment, removes this class of problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you stop bad data reaching a model?&quot; Define a schema and expectations, run them on every batch before training and before serving, version the datasets, and make the pipeline fail closed with an alert instead of continuing with bad rows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Training on data that was never validated\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overwriting a dataset in place\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using future information in a training feature\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing features twice, once for training and once for serving\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"treat-data-like-code-and-like-an-api","text":"Treat data like code and like an API"},{"id":"useful-checks","text":"Useful checks"},{"id":"version-the-data","text":"Version the data"},{"id":"point-in-time-correctness","text":"Point-in-time correctness"},{"id":"one-definition-of-a-feature","text":"One definition of a feature"},{"id":"a-data-check-you-can-run-in-a-pipeline","text":"A data check you can run in a pipeline"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-feature-that-knew-the-future","text":"Incident: The feature that knew the future"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google: Rules of Machine Learning","u":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/rules-of-ml"},{"t":"Evidently documentation","u":"https://docs.evidentlyai.com/"}],"text":"Most machine learning incidents start in the data, not in the model. A feature pipeline is a production system like any other, with inputs that change, jobs that fail and schemas that drift. This lesson covers how to check data before it is used, how to version it so a result can be reproduced, and how to compute features once so training and serving agree. What you will learn Validate data against a schema and expectations before training Version datasets so a model can be traced to its data Explain what a feature store is for and when you do not need one Keep training and serving features computed by the same code Treat data like code and like an API Data has a shape: columns, types, ranges and meanings. Write that shape down as a schema and test every batch against it. A column that is suddenly 40 percent null, a value outside a plausible range, or a new category should stop the pipeline or raise an alert, just as a failing unit test stops a build. Useful checks Start with a short list: row count within an expected band, no unexpected nulls, values within range, categories from a known set, no duplicate keys, freshness (the newest record is recent), and a distribution check against a reference. These catch most real problems and run in seconds. Version the data If you cannot say which data trained a model, you cannot reproduce it or explain a bad result. Store datasets as immutable snapshots with an id, or use a tool that records content hashes next to your code commit. Never overwrite a training set in place. Point-in-time correctness When you build training rows, each feature must use only information that existed at the time of the label. Using today&#39;s value to predict yesterday&#39;s outcome leaks the future and makes the model look great in testing and fail in production. Join features as of the event time. One definition of a feature Write each feature once and use the same code for the training set and for serving. A feature store is a managed way to do that: it keeps an offline copy for training and an online copy for low-latency lookups, with one definition. Small teams can start with a shared library and add a store later. A data check you can run in a pipeline # checks.yaml: expectations for the daily training table table: churn_training checks: - row_count: { min: 90000 , max: 130000 } - freshness: { column: event_date , max_age_hours: 30 } - not_null: [ customer_id , plan_tier , event_date ] - unique: [ customer_id , event_date ] - range: { column: days_since_login , min: 0 , max: 3650 } - allowed_values: { column: plan_tier , values: [ free , basic , pro , team ]} - null_rate: { column: last_payment_amount , max: 0.05 } on_fail: stop_pipeline_and_page Each line is a test on data, evaluated before training starts. A failing check stops the pipeline instead of training on bad rows. How it fails A schema changes upstream and the pipeline keeps running. A join silently drops half the rows. A feature uses information from after the event. The training set is edited by hand and nobody recorded how. A fix is applied to serving but not to training. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Data and feature pipelines for ML fits together Flow in order: 1, Treat data like code and like an API to Useful checks; 2, Useful checks to Version the data; 3, Version the data to Point-in-time correctness; 4, Point-in-time correctness to One definition of a feature; 5, One definition of a feature to A data check you can run in a. Data store Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Treat data like code and like an API Data store What it does: Data has a shape: columns, types, ranges and meanings. If it fails or is weak: Training on data that was never validated. Useful checks Observa"},"mlops/experiment-tracking-and-reproducibility":{"html":"\u003cp>Training a model is closer to running experiments than to building a feature. You try settings, compare numbers and keep the best. Without records, the best model becomes a mystery: nobody remembers which data, code or parameters produced it. Experiment tracking turns that mess into a searchable history, and reproducibility means you can run the same recipe again and get the same result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>List what to record for every training run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain why runs should be compared on the same data and metric\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Control randomness so a result can be repeated\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Link a model in production back to its run\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-record\">What to record\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For every run, record the code version, the data version, the parameters, the environment (library versions, hardware), the random seed, the metrics, and the output files. Together these are the recipe. If any one is missing, you can look at a number but you cannot trust or repeat it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-tracking-server\">A tracking server\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tools such as MLflow, or any system that stores runs, give you a place to log parameters and metrics from code and then compare runs in a table or chart. The tool matters less than the habit: every training job writes a run record automatically, with no manual notes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compare-fairly\">Compare fairly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A new model beating an old one means little if they used different data splits or metrics. Fix the evaluation set and the metric, keep it out of training, and compare on the same slice. Report the metric on important sub-groups too, because an average can hide a group the model serves badly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-randomness\">Control randomness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Random seeds, data shuffling and some GPU operations make results vary between runs. Set seeds, pin library versions and record hardware. Some noise cannot be removed, so run important comparisons several times and look at the spread. A one-point gain inside the noise is not a gain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reproduce-on-demand\">Reproduce on demand\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Package training in a container with pinned dependencies, run it from a commit, and read data by version id. Then &quot;retrain model 14 exactly&quot; becomes a command, not an archaeology project. This also makes audits and rollbacks safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logging-a-run\">Logging a run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mlflow run . -P learning_rate=0.05 -P max_depth=6 --env-manager=local\nmlflow runs compare 7c1e9a40 3b88d216\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlflow run . -P learning_rate=0.05 -P max_depth=6 --env-manager=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">run_id: 7c1e9a40\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">code_version: a94f3c2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">data_version: churn-2026-03-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">seed: 42\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">metric auc_valid: 0.871\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">metric auc_valid_new_customers: 0.804\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">artifact: model/ (12 MB)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlflow runs compare 7c1e9a40 3b88d216\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One command trains and records code, data, seed and metrics together. The new-customer slice is logged too, so an average does not hide a weak group. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Results live in a notebook that was changed after the run. The data path points at a file that was overwritten. A seed is unset, so nobody can repeat the number. Two people compare runs on different test sets and argue about it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Experiment tracking and reproducibility fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What to record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A tracking server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Compare fairly\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control randomness\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reproduce on demand\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logging a run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What to record to A tracking server; 2, A tracking server to Compare fairly; 3, Compare fairly to Control randomness; 4, Control randomness to Reproduce on demand; 5, Reproduce on demand to Logging a run.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What to record \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> For every run, record the code version, the data version, the parameters, the environment (library versions, hardware), the random seed, the metrics, and the output...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaving seeds and library versions unrecorded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>A tracking server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tools such as MLflow, or any system that stores runs, give you a place to log parameters and metrics from code and then compare runs in a table or chart.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Keeping results in notebooks with no run record.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Compare fairly \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A new model beating an old one means little if they used different data splits or metrics.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Comparing runs on different test sets.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Control randomness \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Random seeds, data shuffling and some GPU operations make results vary between runs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Choosing a model on a difference smaller than the noise.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Reproduce on demand \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Package training in a container with pinned dependencies, run it from a commit, and read data by version id.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Logging a run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One command trains and records code, data, seed and metrics together.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-best-model-nobody-could-rebuild\">Incident: The best model nobody could rebuild\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team deployed the best model from weeks of experiments. When it needed retraining, the new run was much worse. The notebook had been edited, the training file replaced and the library upgraded, and nobody had recorded the original settings.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python train.py --config best.yaml\npython compare.py --old model-prod --new run-latest\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python train.py --config best.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">auc_valid: 0.842\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python compare.py --old model-prod --new run-latest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">code version: unknown for model-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">data version: unknown for model-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration of a very common situation. Recording code, data, parameters and environment for every run would have made the old result repeatable and the gap explainable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you make an ML result reproducible?&quot; Version code and data, pin the environment in a container, fix seeds, log parameters and metrics to a tracking system, and evaluate on a fixed held-out set.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keeping results in notebooks with no run record\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Comparing runs on different test sets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving seeds and library versions unrecorded\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a model on a difference smaller than the noise\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-record","text":"What to record"},{"id":"a-tracking-server","text":"A tracking server"},{"id":"compare-fairly","text":"Compare fairly"},{"id":"control-randomness","text":"Control randomness"},{"id":"reproduce-on-demand","text":"Reproduce on demand"},{"id":"logging-a-run","text":"Logging a run"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-best-model-nobody-could-rebuild","text":"Incident: The best model nobody could rebuild"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"MLflow Tracking","u":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/tracking.html"},{"t":"MLflow documentation","u":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/index.html"}],"text":"Training a model is closer to running experiments than to building a feature. You try settings, compare numbers and keep the best. Without records, the best model becomes a mystery: nobody remembers which data, code or parameters produced it. Experiment tracking turns that mess into a searchable history, and reproducibility means you can run the same recipe again and get the same result. What you will learn List what to record for every training run Explain why runs should be compared on the same data and metric Control randomness so a result can be repeated Link a model in production back to its run What to record For every run, record the code version, the data version, the parameters, the environment (library versions, hardware), the random seed, the metrics, and the output files. Together these are the recipe. If any one is missing, you can look at a number but you cannot trust or repeat it. A tracking server Tools such as MLflow, or any system that stores runs, give you a place to log parameters and metrics from code and then compare runs in a table or chart. The tool matters less than the habit: every training job writes a run record automatically, with no manual notes. Compare fairly A new model beating an old one means little if they used different data splits or metrics. Fix the evaluation set and the metric, keep it out of training, and compare on the same slice. Report the metric on important sub-groups too, because an average can hide a group the model serves badly. Control randomness Random seeds, data shuffling and some GPU operations make results vary between runs. Set seeds, pin library versions and record hardware. Some noise cannot be removed, so run important comparisons several times and look at the spread. A one-point gain inside the noise is not a gain. Reproduce on demand Package training in a container with pinned dependencies, run it from a commit, and read data by version id. Then \"retrain model 14 exactly\" becomes a command, not an archaeology project. This also makes audits and rollbacks safe. Logging a run $ mlflow run . -P learning_rate=0.05 -P max_depth=6 --env-manager= local run_id: 7c1e9a40 code_version: a94f3c2 data_version: churn-2026-03-01 seed: 42 metric auc_valid: 0.871 metric auc_valid_new_customers: 0.804 artifact: model/ (12 MB) $ mlflow runs compare 7c1e9a40 3b88d216 One command trains and records code, data, seed and metrics together. The new-customer slice is logged too, so an average does not hide a weak group. The output is a simulated illustration. How it fails Results live in a notebook that was changed after the run. The data path points at a file that was overwritten. A seed is unset, so nobody can repeat the number. Two people compare runs on different test sets and argue about it. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Experiment tracking and reproducibility fits together Flow in order: 1, What to record to A tracking server; 2, A tracking server to Compare fairly; 3, Compare fairly to Control randomness; 4, Control randomness to Reproduce on demand; 5, Reproduce on demand to Logging a run. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What to record Step What it does: For every run, record the code version, the data version, the parameters, the environment (library versions, hardware), the random seed, the metrics, and the output... If it fails or is weak: Leaving seeds and library versions unrecorded. A tracking server Step What it does: Tools such as MLflow, or any system that stores runs, give you a place to log parameters and metrics from code and then compare runs in a table or chart. If it fails or is weak: Keeping results in notebooks with no run record. Compare fairly Step What it does: A new model beating an old one means li"},"mlops/training-pipelines-and-testing-models-in-ci":{"html":"\u003cp>A model trained by hand from a laptop cannot be released safely or repeated reliably. A training pipeline runs the same steps every time: fetch data, validate, build features, train, evaluate and package. Continuous integration then adds tests, so a change to code or data cannot ship a worse model without somebody seeing it first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Describe the stages of a training pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write tests for data, code and model quality\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use an evaluation gate to block a worse model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run training jobs in containers with the right resources\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-stages\">The stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A typical pipeline has these stages: ingest data, validate it, build features, train, evaluate, register the model and notify. Each stage reads versioned inputs and writes versioned outputs, so a failed stage can be rerun without redoing everything and any result can be traced.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-kinds-of-tests\">Three kinds of tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Test the code with ordinary unit tests, for example that feature functions give expected results on small inputs. Test the data with expectations on every batch. Test the model: it must load, accept the expected input shape, return outputs in a valid range and meet a minimum quality bar on a fixed evaluation set.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-evaluation-gate\">The evaluation gate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before a model is registered, compare it with the current production model on the same held-out data. Block the release if the key metric is worse by more than a small margin, or if any important group gets worse. A gate turns &quot;I think it is better&quot; into a measured rule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"behavioural-checks\">Behavioural checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add simple behaviour tests: a tiny change that should not matter does not flip the answer, an obviously bad input is handled, and known hard cases still work. They are cheap, and they catch failures that a single average hides.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"containers-and-resources\">Containers and resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run each stage in a container with pinned dependencies and explicit CPU, memory and GPU requests. Expensive GPU nodes should run only the training step, while cheap nodes run validation and packaging. Add timeouts and retries so one stuck job does not block the queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-pipeline-with-an-evaluation-gate\">A pipeline with an evaluation gate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"name: train-churn-model\non:\n  schedule: [{cron: &quot;0 2 * * 1&quot;}]\n  push: {paths: [&quot;features/**&quot;, &quot;train/**&quot;]}\njobs:\n  validate-data:\n    run: python -m checks.run --config checks.yaml\n  train:\n    needs: validate-data\n    resources: {gpu: 1, memory: 16Gi}\n    run: python train/train.py --data-version $DATA_VERSION --seed 42\n  evaluate:\n    needs: train\n    run: &gt;\n      python eval/gate.py --candidate $RUN_ID --baseline prod\n      --metric auc --min-delta -0.002 --groups new_customers,enterprise\n  register:\n    needs: evaluate\n    run: python registry/register.py --run $RUN_ID --stage staging\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">train-churn-model\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">on:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">schedule:\u003c/span> [{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cron:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0 2 * * 1&quot;\u003c/span>}]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">push:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;features/**&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;train/**&quot;\u003c/span>]}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">validate-data:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">checks.run\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--config\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">checks.yaml\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">train:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">needs:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">validate-data\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">gpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">16Gi\u003c/span>}\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">train/train.py\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--data-version\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$DATA_VERSION\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--seed\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">42\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">evaluate:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">needs:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">train\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&gt;\n      python eval/gate.py --candidate $RUN_ID --baseline prod\n      --metric auc --min-delta -0.002 --groups new_customers,enterprise\n\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">register:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">needs:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">evaluate\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry/register.py\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--run\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$RUN_ID\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--stage\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">staging\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each job depends on the one before it. The gate allows a tiny drop in the overall metric but blocks any release that makes a named group worse, so a bad model never reaches the registry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pipeline trains on unvalidated data. A gate that only looks at the average lets a model that fails one customer group through. Training and serving use different library versions. A failed job is retried forever and fills the GPU queue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Training pipelines and testing models in CI fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The stages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Three kinds of tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">The evaluation gate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Behavioural checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Containers and resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A pipeline with an evaluation gate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, The stages to Three kinds of tests; 2, Three kinds of tests to The evaluation gate; 3, The evaluation gate to Behavioural checks; 4, Behavioural checks to Containers and resources; 5, Containers and resources to A pipeline with an evaluation gate.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>The stages \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A typical pipeline has these stages: ingest data, validate it, build features, train, evaluate, register the model and notify.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retrying failed GPU jobs without limits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Three kinds of tests \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Test the code with ordinary unit tests, for example that feature functions give expected results on small inputs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>The evaluation gate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Before a model is registered, compare it with the current production model on the same held-out data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Evaluating only on the average metric.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Behavioural checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add simple behaviour tests: a tiny change that should not matter does not flip the answer, an obviously bad input is handled, and known hard cases still work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Containers and resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run each stage in a container with pinned dependencies and explicit CPU, memory and GPU requests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Training and serving in different environments.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A pipeline with an evaluation gate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Each job depends on the one before it. The gate allows a tiny drop in the overall metric but blocks any release that makes a named group worse, so a bad model never...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No gate between training and the registry.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-retrain-that-shipped-a-worse-model\">Incident: The retrain that shipped a worse model\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A weekly retraining job replaced the production model automatically. One week, a data problem lowered quality for new customers. The average metric barely moved, so nothing flagged it, and the support team noticed first.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python eval/gate.py --candidate run-88 --baseline prod --metric auc\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">eval\u003c/span>/gate.py --candidate run-88 --baseline prod --metric auc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">overall auc: 0.866 (prod 0.869)  within tolerance\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">new_customers auc: 0.741 (prod 0.803)  BELOW LIMIT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">result: FAIL, not registered\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. Checking named groups in the gate, not only the average, turns a quiet regression into a failed build before it reaches users.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How would you automate model releases safely?&quot; Build a pipeline with data checks, tests, a fixed evaluation set, a comparison against the production model that includes important groups, and an approval or canary step before full release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Evaluating only on the average metric\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Training and serving in different environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No gate between training and the registry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying failed GPU jobs without limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-stages","text":"The stages"},{"id":"three-kinds-of-tests","text":"Three kinds of tests"},{"id":"the-evaluation-gate","text":"The evaluation gate"},{"id":"behavioural-checks","text":"Behavioural checks"},{"id":"containers-and-resources","text":"Containers and resources"},{"id":"a-pipeline-with-an-evaluation-gate","text":"A pipeline with an evaluation gate"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-retrain-that-shipped-a-worse-model","text":"Incident: The retrain that shipped a worse model"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubeflow documentation","u":"https://www.kubeflow.org/docs/"},{"t":"Google Cloud: MLOps pipelines","u":"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/mlops-continuous-delivery-and-automation-pipelines-in-machine-learning"}],"text":"A model trained by hand from a laptop cannot be released safely or repeated reliably. A training pipeline runs the same steps every time: fetch data, validate, build features, train, evaluate and package. Continuous integration then adds tests, so a change to code or data cannot ship a worse model without somebody seeing it first. What you will learn Describe the stages of a training pipeline Write tests for data, code and model quality Use an evaluation gate to block a worse model Run training jobs in containers with the right resources The stages A typical pipeline has these stages: ingest data, validate it, build features, train, evaluate, register the model and notify. Each stage reads versioned inputs and writes versioned outputs, so a failed stage can be rerun without redoing everything and any result can be traced. Three kinds of tests Test the code with ordinary unit tests, for example that feature functions give expected results on small inputs. Test the data with expectations on every batch. Test the model: it must load, accept the expected input shape, return outputs in a valid range and meet a minimum quality bar on a fixed evaluation set. The evaluation gate Before a model is registered, compare it with the current production model on the same held-out data. Block the release if the key metric is worse by more than a small margin, or if any important group gets worse. A gate turns \"I think it is better\" into a measured rule. Behavioural checks Add simple behaviour tests: a tiny change that should not matter does not flip the answer, an obviously bad input is handled, and known hard cases still work. They are cheap, and they catch failures that a single average hides. Containers and resources Run each stage in a container with pinned dependencies and explicit CPU, memory and GPU requests. Expensive GPU nodes should run only the training step, while cheap nodes run validation and packaging. Add timeouts and retries so one stuck job does not block the queue. A pipeline with an evaluation gate name: train-churn-model on: schedule: [{ cron: \"0 2 * * 1\" }] push: { paths: [ \"features/**\" , \"train/**\" ]} jobs: validate-data: run: python -m checks.run --config checks.yaml train: needs: validate-data resources: { gpu: 1 , memory: 16Gi } run: python train/train.py --data-version $DATA_VERSION --seed 42 evaluate: needs: train run: > python eval/gate.py --candidate $RUN_ID --baseline prod --metric auc --min-delta -0.002 --groups new_customers,enterprise register: needs: evaluate run: python registry/register.py --run $RUN_ID --stage staging Each job depends on the one before it. The gate allows a tiny drop in the overall metric but blocks any release that makes a named group worse, so a bad model never reaches the registry. How it fails A pipeline trains on unvalidated data. A gate that only looks at the average lets a model that fails one customer group through. Training and serving use different library versions. A failed job is retried forever and fills the GPU queue. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Training pipelines and testing models in CI fits together Flow in order: 1, The stages to Three kinds of tests; 2, Three kinds of tests to The evaluation gate; 3, The evaluation gate to Behavioural checks; 4, Behavioural checks to Containers and resources; 5, Containers and resources to A pipeline with an evaluation gate. Step Observability Queue Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. The stages Step What it does: A typical pipeline has these stages: ingest data, validate it, build features, train, evaluate, register the model and notify. If it fails or is weak: Retrying failed GPU jobs without limits. Three kinds of tests Observability What it does: Test the code with ordinary unit tests, fo"},"mlops/model-registry-packaging-and-safe-releases":{"html":"\u003cp>Once a model passes its checks, it needs a home and a safe path to users. A model registry gives each version a name, a lineage and a stage. Packaging makes the model run the same way everywhere. A careful release then exposes the model to real traffic in small steps, with a quick way back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Describe what a model registry stores and why\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Package a model with its dependencies and interface\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compare shadow, canary and A/B release methods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roll back a model in minutes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-registry-holds\">What a registry holds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A registry stores each model version with its run id, code and data versions, metrics, owner, and a stage such as staging, production or archived. It answers &quot;what is live, who approved it, what trained it and what was the previous one?&quot; in one place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"package-it-like-a-service\">Package it like a service\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Wrap the model in a container with pinned libraries, a clear input schema and a health endpoint. Include the exact feature code or call it from a shared library. Record the image digest in the registry, so the thing you tested is the thing you run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shadow-release\">Shadow release\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In shadow mode the new model receives a copy of real traffic, but its answers are only logged, not used. You compare it with the live model on real inputs with zero user risk. It also shows latency and error rates under real load.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"canary-and-a-b\">Canary and A/B\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A canary sends a small share of traffic, say 1 to 5 percent, to the new model and watches errors, latency and quality metrics before widening. An A/B test goes further and measures a business outcome between groups, which needs enough traffic and time to be meaningful.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollback\">Rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep the previous model loaded or one command away. Make rollback a registry stage change, not a rebuild. Decide the triggers in advance: error rate, latency, a quality proxy or a business metric beyond a limit. Practise it before you need it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"moving-a-model-through-stages\">Moving a model through stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mlreg show churn --stage production\nmlreg promote churn 14 --to staging\ndeploy canary churn --version 14 --share 5%\nmlreg promote churn 14 --to production --keep-previous 13\nmlreg rollback churn --to 13   # if a trigger fires\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlreg show churn --stage production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">version: 13  image: registry.example.com/churn@sha256:4f2a...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">trained: run 3b88d216  data: churn-2026-02-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlreg promote churn 14 --to staging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>deploy canary churn --version 14 --share 5%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">canary: error_rate 0.1% latency_p95 44ms (baseline 41ms)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlreg promote churn 14 --to production --keep-previous 13\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlreg rollback churn --to 13   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># if a trigger fires\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The registry holds the version, the image digest and the previous version. A canary at five percent is checked before promotion. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The model file is copied by hand to a server. A release has no previous version to return to. The canary watches only latency, not quality. Everyone assumes someone else approved the model.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Model registry, packaging and safe releases fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What a registry holds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Package it like a service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shadow release\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Canary and A/B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Moving a model through stages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, What a registry holds to Package it like a service; 2, Package it like a service to Shadow release; 3, Shadow release to Canary and A/B; 4, Canary and A/B to Rollback; 5, Rollback to Moving a model through stages.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>What a registry holds \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A registry stores each model version with its run id, code and data versions, metrics, owner, and a stage such as staging, production or archived.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No named owner or approval for a model.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Package it like a service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Wrap the model in a container with pinned libraries, a clear input schema and a health endpoint.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Copying model files by hand.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Shadow release \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> In shadow mode the new model receives a copy of real traffic, but its answers are only logged, not used.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Canarying with only latency checks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Canary and A/B \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A canary sends a small share of traffic, say 1 to 5 percent, to the new model and watches errors, latency and quality metrics before widening.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Rollback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keep the previous model loaded or one command away.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>Moving a model through stages \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The registry holds the version, the image digest and the previous version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-rollback-that-took-a-day\">Incident: A rollback that took a day\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new model started giving poor results in production. The team wanted to go back, but the old model had been deleted from the server and its training environment no longer existed. Rebuilding it took most of a day.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mlreg show churn --stage archived\nmlreg rollback churn --to 13\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlreg show churn --stage archived\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">version: 13  artifact: MISSING\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mlreg rollback churn --to 13\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: artifact not found, rebuild required\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. Keeping the previous model artifact and its image in the registry, and treating rollback as a stage change, turns an all-day rebuild into a minute-long switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you release a new model safely?&quot; Register it with lineage, package it, run shadow traffic, then a small canary with quality and latency checks, promote in steps, and keep the previous version ready for an instant rollback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Copying model files by hand\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting old versions after release\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canarying with only latency checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No named owner or approval for a model\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-a-registry-holds","text":"What a registry holds"},{"id":"package-it-like-a-service","text":"Package it like a service"},{"id":"shadow-release","text":"Shadow release"},{"id":"canary-and-a-b","text":"Canary and A/B"},{"id":"rollback","text":"Rollback"},{"id":"moving-a-model-through-stages","text":"Moving a model through stages"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-a-rollback-that-took-a-day","text":"Incident: A rollback that took a day"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"MLflow Model Registry","u":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/model-registry.html"},{"t":"Azure Machine Learning: model management and deployment","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/machine-learning/concept-model-management-and-deployment"}],"text":"Once a model passes its checks, it needs a home and a safe path to users. A model registry gives each version a name, a lineage and a stage. Packaging makes the model run the same way everywhere. A careful release then exposes the model to real traffic in small steps, with a quick way back. What you will learn Describe what a model registry stores and why Package a model with its dependencies and interface Compare shadow, canary and A/B release methods Roll back a model in minutes What a registry holds A registry stores each model version with its run id, code and data versions, metrics, owner, and a stage such as staging, production or archived. It answers \"what is live, who approved it, what trained it and what was the previous one?\" in one place. Package it like a service Wrap the model in a container with pinned libraries, a clear input schema and a health endpoint. Include the exact feature code or call it from a shared library. Record the image digest in the registry, so the thing you tested is the thing you run. Shadow release In shadow mode the new model receives a copy of real traffic, but its answers are only logged, not used. You compare it with the live model on real inputs with zero user risk. It also shows latency and error rates under real load. Canary and A/B A canary sends a small share of traffic, say 1 to 5 percent, to the new model and watches errors, latency and quality metrics before widening. An A/B test goes further and measures a business outcome between groups, which needs enough traffic and time to be meaningful. Rollback Keep the previous model loaded or one command away. Make rollback a registry stage change, not a rebuild. Decide the triggers in advance: error rate, latency, a quality proxy or a business metric beyond a limit. Practise it before you need it. Moving a model through stages $ mlreg show churn --stage production version: 13 image: registry.example.com/churn@sha256:4f2a... trained: run 3b88d216 data: churn-2026-02-01 $ mlreg promote churn 14 --to staging $ deploy canary churn --version 14 --share 5% canary: error_rate 0.1% latency_p95 44ms (baseline 41ms) $ mlreg promote churn 14 --to production --keep-previous 13 $ mlreg rollback churn --to 13 # if a trigger fires The registry holds the version, the image digest and the previous version. A canary at five percent is checked before promotion. The output is a simulated illustration. How it fails The model file is copied by hand to a server. A release has no previous version to return to. The canary watches only latency, not quality. Everyone assumes someone else approved the model. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Model registry, packaging and safe releases fits together Flow in order: 1, What a registry holds to Package it like a service; 2, Package it like a service to Shadow release; 3, Shadow release to Canary and A/B; 4, Canary and A/B to Rollback; 5, Rollback to Moving a model through stages. Outside party Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. What a registry holds Outside party What it does: A registry stores each model version with its run id, code and data versions, metrics, owner, and a stage such as staging, production or archived. If it fails or is weak: No named owner or approval for a model. Package it like a service Step What it does: Wrap the model in a container with pinned libraries, a clear input schema and a health endpoint. If it fails or is weak: Copying model files by hand. Shadow release Step What it does: In shadow mode the new model receives a copy of real traffic, but its answers are only logged, not used. If it fails or is weak: Canarying with only latency checks. Canary and A/B Step What it does: A canary sends a small share of traffic, say 1 to 5 percent, to the new "},"mlops/serving-models-batch-online-and-streaming":{"html":"\u003cp>There are several ways to turn a model into predictions, and the right one depends on how fast an answer is needed. Overnight scoring, a request-response API and a stream of events each have different costs and failure modes. This lesson helps you pick one, and shows what to measure so the service stays fast and affordable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose between batch, online and streaming serving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reason about latency, throughput and batching\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scale and protect a model service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle slow, large or failing model calls\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"batch\">Batch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Batch scoring runs the model over a large table on a schedule, then stores the results for other systems to read. It is cheap and simple, and it suits recommendations refreshed daily or risk scores for reports. The cost is staleness: the answer is as old as the last run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"online\">Online\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Online serving answers one request at a time through an API, usually in tens to hundreds of milliseconds. It suits fraud checks, search ranking and anything that depends on the latest event. It needs capacity for peaks, health checks, timeouts and a fallback when the model is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"streaming\">Streaming\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Streaming scores events as they arrive from a queue. It sits between the two: near real-time, high volume and decoupled from the caller. You must plan for ordering, duplicates, backlog and what happens when the model is slower than the incoming rate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"latency-and-batching\">Latency and batching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Model inference often runs much faster per item when several requests are grouped. Dynamic batching waits a few milliseconds to collect requests and then runs them together. It raises throughput but adds a little delay, so set a maximum wait that fits your latency budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scale-and-protect\">Scale and protect\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Autoscale on a signal that reflects load, such as queue depth or concurrent requests, not only CPU. GPU services start slowly because models are large, so keep a warm minimum. Set timeouts, limit concurrency, and return a safe default or an older cached answer when the model cannot respond.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-model-service-with-sensible-limits\">A model service with sensible limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: {name: churn-model}\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector: {matchLabels: {app: churn-model}}\n  template:\n    metadata: {labels: {app: churn-model}}\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: server\n        image: registry.example.com/churn@sha256:4f2a1c\n        resources:\n          requests: {cpu: &quot;2&quot;, memory: 4Gi}\n          limits: {memory: 6Gi}\n        readinessProbe:\n          httpGet: {path: /ready, port: 8080}\n          initialDelaySeconds: 20\n        env:\n        - {name: MAX_BATCH_SIZE, value: &quot;16&quot;}\n        - {name: MAX_BATCH_WAIT_MS, value: &quot;8&quot;}\n        - {name: REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS, value: &quot;250&quot;}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">churn-model\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">churn-model\u003c/span>}}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">churn-model\u003c/span>}}\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">server\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/churn@sha256:4f2a1c\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">4Gi\u003c/span>}\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">6Gi\u003c/span>}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readinessProbe:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/ready\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>}\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">initialDelaySeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">MAX_BATCH_SIZE\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;16&quot;\u003c/span>}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">MAX_BATCH_WAIT_MS\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;8&quot;\u003c/span>}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;250&quot;\u003c/span>}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The readiness probe waits for the model to load. Batching is capped at eight milliseconds and a request timeout protects callers. Set these from your latency budget, not by guesswork.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A model takes minutes to load, so new pods receive traffic too early. Batching waits too long and breaks the latency budget. A traffic spike overloads the service and callers pile up retries. No fallback exists when the model is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Serving models: batch, online and streaming fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Batch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Online\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Streaming\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Latency and batching\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scale and protect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A model service with sensible limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Batch to Online; 2, Online to Streaming; 3, Streaming to Latency and batching; 4, Latency and batching to Scale and protect; 5, Scale and protect to A model service with sensible limits.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Batch \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Batch scoring runs the model over a large table on a schedule, then stores the results for other systems to read.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> CPU-only autoscaling for a GPU model.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Online \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Online serving answers one request at a time through an API, usually in tens to hundreds of milliseconds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Online serving for something that needs no freshness.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Streaming \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Streaming scores events as they arrive from a queue.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Serving traffic before the model has loaded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Latency and batching \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Model inference often runs much faster per item when several requests are grouped.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timeout or fallback for the model call.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Scale and protect \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Autoscale on a signal that reflects load, such as queue depth or concurrent requests, not only CPU.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Set timeouts, limit concurrency, and return a safe default or an older cached answer when the model cannot respond.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A model service with sensible limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The readiness probe waits for the model to load.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-retries-that-finished-the-job\">Incident: Retries that finished the job\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model service slowed down under load. Callers timed out and retried, which doubled the load and made it slower still, until the service stopped answering. The team had no concurrency limit and no fallback.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top pod -l app=churn-model\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&quot; localhost:8080/predict\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pod -l app=churn-model\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            CPU    MEMORY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">churn-model-0   3980m  5.1Gi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%{http_code} %{time_total}\\n&quot;\u003c/span> localhost:8080/predict\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">503 4.201\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration of a retry storm. A concurrency limit, a retry budget with backoff on callers, and a cheap fallback answer keep a slow model from taking down everything that depends on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Batch or online for a recommendation feature?&quot; Ask how fresh the answer must be. If daily is fine, batch is cheaper and simpler. If it must react to the last click, use online with a fallback to the batch result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Online serving for something that needs no freshness\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU-only autoscaling for a GPU model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Serving traffic before the model has loaded\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeout or fallback for the model call\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"batch","text":"Batch"},{"id":"online","text":"Online"},{"id":"streaming","text":"Streaming"},{"id":"latency-and-batching","text":"Latency and batching"},{"id":"scale-and-protect","text":"Scale and protect"},{"id":"a-model-service-with-sensible-limits","text":"A model service with sensible limits"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-retries-that-finished-the-job","text":"Incident: Retries that finished the job"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon SageMaker: deploy models","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/deploy-model.html"},{"t":"Kubernetes: scheduling GPUs","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-gpus/scheduling-gpus/"}],"text":"There are several ways to turn a model into predictions, and the right one depends on how fast an answer is needed. Overnight scoring, a request-response API and a stream of events each have different costs and failure modes. This lesson helps you pick one, and shows what to measure so the service stays fast and affordable. What you will learn Choose between batch, online and streaming serving Reason about latency, throughput and batching Scale and protect a model service Handle slow, large or failing model calls Batch Batch scoring runs the model over a large table on a schedule, then stores the results for other systems to read. It is cheap and simple, and it suits recommendations refreshed daily or risk scores for reports. The cost is staleness: the answer is as old as the last run. Online Online serving answers one request at a time through an API, usually in tens to hundreds of milliseconds. It suits fraud checks, search ranking and anything that depends on the latest event. It needs capacity for peaks, health checks, timeouts and a fallback when the model is down. Streaming Streaming scores events as they arrive from a queue. It sits between the two: near real-time, high volume and decoupled from the caller. You must plan for ordering, duplicates, backlog and what happens when the model is slower than the incoming rate. Latency and batching Model inference often runs much faster per item when several requests are grouped. Dynamic batching waits a few milliseconds to collect requests and then runs them together. It raises throughput but adds a little delay, so set a maximum wait that fits your latency budget. Scale and protect Autoscale on a signal that reflects load, such as queue depth or concurrent requests, not only CPU. GPU services start slowly because models are large, so keep a warm minimum. Set timeouts, limit concurrency, and return a safe default or an older cached answer when the model cannot respond. A model service with sensible limits apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: { name: churn-model } spec: replicas: 3 selector: { matchLabels: { app: churn-model }} template: metadata: { labels: { app: churn-model }} spec: containers: - name: server image: registry.example.com/churn@sha256:4f2a1c resources: requests: { cpu: \"2\" , memory: 4Gi } limits: { memory: 6Gi } readinessProbe: httpGet: { path: /ready , port: 8080 } initialDelaySeconds: 20 env: - { name: MAX_BATCH_SIZE , value: \"16\" } - { name: MAX_BATCH_WAIT_MS , value: \"8\" } - { name: REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS , value: \"250\" } The readiness probe waits for the model to load. Batching is capped at eight milliseconds and a request timeout protects callers. Set these from your latency budget, not by guesswork. How it fails A model takes minutes to load, so new pods receive traffic too early. Batching waits too long and breaks the latency budget. A traffic spike overloads the service and callers pile up retries. No fallback exists when the model is down. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Serving models: batch, online and streaming fits together Flow in order: 1, Batch to Online; 2, Online to Streaming; 3, Streaming to Latency and batching; 4, Latency and batching to Scale and protect; 5, Scale and protect to A model service with sensible limits. Step Queue Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Batch Step What it does: Batch scoring runs the model over a large table on a schedule, then stores the results for other systems to read. If it fails or is weak: CPU-only autoscaling for a GPU model. Online Step What it does: Online serving answers one request at a time through an API, usually in tens to hundreds of milliseconds. If it fails or is weak: Online serving for something that needs no freshness. Streaming Queue What it does:"},"mlops/monitoring-drift-and-retraining":{"html":"\u003cp>A model is trained on a snapshot of the past. The world keeps moving, so over time the data it sees and the patterns it learned drift apart. Monitoring tells you when. A retraining plan tells you what to do. This final lesson ties the earlier ones together into the loop that keeps a model useful.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tell data drift from concept drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose signals to monitor when labels arrive late\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set alert thresholds that do not cry wolf\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design a safe retraining trigger and process\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-drift-and-concept-drift\">Data drift and concept drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Data drift means the inputs changed: more mobile users, a new product, a different season. Concept drift means the relationship between inputs and the right answer changed: customers behave differently for the same features. Data drift is easy to measure. Concept drift needs labels or a trusted proxy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-monitor\">What to monitor\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Watch input features (null rate, range, distribution against a training reference), predictions (average score, share in each class) and, when labels arrive, real quality such as precision, recall or error. Add the business metric the model serves. Group results by important segments.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-labels-are-late\">When labels are late\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Labels often arrive days or weeks later: a loan defaults months after approval. Until then, rely on leading signals: input drift, prediction drift and proxies. Plan a delayed quality report when labels land, and compare it with what the early signals said.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"thresholds-and-noise\">Thresholds and noise\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Distribution tests will flag tiny, harmless shifts if you run them on large data. Pick a few features that matter, choose a drift measure with a meaningful scale, and alert on sustained shifts over a window rather than one-off blips. Tune using past incidents, so alerts match real harm.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retraining\">Retraining\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Retrain on a schedule, on a trigger such as drift or a quality drop, or both. Every retrain goes through the same pipeline and gate as a new model, including a comparison with production and a staged release. Automatic retraining without a gate is how a data problem turns into a bad release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-drift-alert-rule\">A drift alert rule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"groups:\n- name: model-drift\n  rules:\n  - alert: FeatureDriftHigh\n    expr: model_feature_psi{model=&quot;churn&quot;,feature=&quot;days_since_login&quot;} &gt; 0.25\n    for: 6h\n    labels: {severity: ticket}\n    annotations:\n      summary: &quot;Input drift on days_since_login for 6 hours&quot;\n      runbook: &quot;Check upstream pipeline, compare with training reference&quot;\n  - alert: PredictionShare\n    expr: abs(model_positive_rate{model=&quot;churn&quot;} - 0.18) &gt; 0.06\n    for: 12h\n    labels: {severity: ticket}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">groups:\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">model-drift\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">alert:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">FeatureDriftHigh\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expr:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">model_feature_psi{model=&quot;churn&quot;,feature=&quot;days_since_login&quot;}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.25\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">for:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">6h\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">severity:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ticket\u003c/span>}\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">summary:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Input drift on days_since_login for 6 hours&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runbook:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Check upstream pipeline, compare with training reference&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">alert:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PredictionShare\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expr:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">abs(model_positive_rate{model=&quot;churn&quot;}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.18\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.06\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">for:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">12h\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">severity:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ticket\u003c/span>}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first rule uses a population stability index with a six hour window. The second watches the share of positive predictions. Both open a ticket, not a page, because drift is slow and needs investigation, not a midnight wake-up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alerts fire constantly on harmless changes, so people mute them. Nobody watches quality because labels are late. A retrain is triggered automatically with bad data. Drift is measured on features nobody cares about.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Monitoring drift and retraining fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Data drift and concept drift\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">What to monitor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">When labels are late\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Thresholds and noise\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retraining\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A drift alert rule\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Data drift and concept drift to What to monitor; 2, What to monitor to When labels are late; 3, When labels are late to Thresholds and noise; 4, Thresholds and noise to Retraining; 5, Retraining to A drift alert rule.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Data drift and concept drift \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Data drift means the inputs changed: more mobile users, a new product, a different season.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>What to monitor \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Watch input features (null rate, range, distribution against a training reference), predictions (average score, share in each class) and, when labels arrive, real...\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring segments that matter to the business.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>When labels are late \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Labels often arrive days or weeks later: a loan defaults months after approval.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retraining automatically with no gate.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Thresholds and noise \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Distribution tests will flag tiny, harmless shifts if you run them on large data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alerting on every tiny distribution change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Retraining \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Retrain on a schedule, on a trigger such as drift or a quality drop, or both.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never comparing the model with a simple baseline.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A drift alert rule \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The first rule uses a population stability index with a six hour window.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-model-that-aged-quietly\">Incident: The model that aged quietly\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A demand forecasting model was accurate at launch. A change in customer behaviour over several months made its forecasts steadily worse. No quality metric was reviewed, and the team found out when stock shortages appeared.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python quality_report.py --model demand --window 90d\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python quality_report.py --model demand --window 90d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">month   mae   baseline_mae\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-01 41    44\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-02 48    44\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-03 63    44   ABOVE BASELINE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. Comparing error with a simple baseline every month, and alerting when the model is no longer better than that baseline, makes slow decay visible while it is still cheap to fix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;How do you know when to retrain?&quot; Monitor input and prediction drift as early signals, measure real quality as labels arrive, compare with a simple baseline, and trigger a retrain through the normal gated pipeline when drift or quality crosses a limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Alerting on every tiny distribution change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retraining automatically with no gate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never comparing the model with a simple baseline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring segments that matter to the business\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"data-drift-and-concept-drift","text":"Data drift and concept drift"},{"id":"what-to-monitor","text":"What to monitor"},{"id":"when-labels-are-late","text":"When labels are late"},{"id":"thresholds-and-noise","text":"Thresholds and noise"},{"id":"retraining","text":"Retraining"},{"id":"a-drift-alert-rule","text":"A drift alert rule"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-model-that-aged-quietly","text":"Incident: The model that aged quietly"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/model-monitor.html"},{"t":"Evidently documentation","u":"https://docs.evidentlyai.com/"}],"text":"A model is trained on a snapshot of the past. The world keeps moving, so over time the data it sees and the patterns it learned drift apart. Monitoring tells you when. A retraining plan tells you what to do. This final lesson ties the earlier ones together into the loop that keeps a model useful. What you will learn Tell data drift from concept drift Choose signals to monitor when labels arrive late Set alert thresholds that do not cry wolf Design a safe retraining trigger and process Data drift and concept drift Data drift means the inputs changed: more mobile users, a new product, a different season. Concept drift means the relationship between inputs and the right answer changed: customers behave differently for the same features. Data drift is easy to measure. Concept drift needs labels or a trusted proxy. What to monitor Watch input features (null rate, range, distribution against a training reference), predictions (average score, share in each class) and, when labels arrive, real quality such as precision, recall or error. Add the business metric the model serves. Group results by important segments. When labels are late Labels often arrive days or weeks later: a loan defaults months after approval. Until then, rely on leading signals: input drift, prediction drift and proxies. Plan a delayed quality report when labels land, and compare it with what the early signals said. Thresholds and noise Distribution tests will flag tiny, harmless shifts if you run them on large data. Pick a few features that matter, choose a drift measure with a meaningful scale, and alert on sustained shifts over a window rather than one-off blips. Tune using past incidents, so alerts match real harm. Retraining Retrain on a schedule, on a trigger such as drift or a quality drop, or both. Every retrain goes through the same pipeline and gate as a new model, including a comparison with production and a staged release. Automatic retraining without a gate is how a data problem turns into a bad release. A drift alert rule groups: - name: model-drift rules: - alert: FeatureDriftHigh expr: model_feature_psi{model=\"churn\",feature=\"days_since_login\"} > 0.25 for: 6h labels: { severity: ticket } annotations: summary: \"Input drift on days_since_login for 6 hours\" runbook: \"Check upstream pipeline, compare with training reference\" - alert: PredictionShare expr: abs(model_positive_rate{model=\"churn\"} - 0.18 ) > 0.06 for: 12h labels: { severity: ticket } The first rule uses a population stability index with a six hour window. The second watches the share of positive predictions. Both open a ticket, not a page, because drift is slow and needs investigation, not a midnight wake-up. How it fails Alerts fire constantly on harmless changes, so people mute them. Nobody watches quality because labels are late. A retrain is triggered automatically with bad data. Drift is measured on features nobody cares about. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Monitoring drift and retraining fits together Flow in order: 1, Data drift and concept drift to What to monitor; 2, What to monitor to When labels are late; 3, When labels are late to Thresholds and noise; 4, Thresholds and noise to Retraining; 5, Retraining to A drift alert rule. Data store Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Data drift and concept drift Data store What it does: Data drift means the inputs changed: more mobile users, a new product, a different season. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. What to monitor Observability What it does: Watch input features (null rate, range, distribution against a training reference), predictions (average score, share in each class) and, when labels arrive"},"mlops/debugging-ml-systems-in-production":{"html":"\u003cp>When a model misbehaves, the model is rarely the first thing to blame. Work from the data upward and change one layer at a time, so that each check rules something out.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Walk a bottom-up checklist: data, features, model, serving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tell training-serving skew from real drift using logged inputs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replay a bad prediction from stored inputs and the model version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write the regression test that stops the same fault returning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-what-changed\">Start with what changed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List what changed in the last week: data sources, feature code, model version, serving image, traffic mix. Most production faults follow a change. If nothing changed, suspect slow drift or an upstream system that changed without telling you.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-the-data-then-the-features\">Check the data, then the features\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compare today&#39;s inputs with the training set: null rates, ranges, new category values, row counts. Then compare the feature values the service computed with the values the training pipeline would compute for the same record. A difference is skew, and skew is a code fault, not a model fault.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replay-the-prediction\">Replay the prediction\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Log the model version, the input features and the output for a sample of requests. With those three you can replay one bad prediction offline, get the same answer, and then change one thing at a time to see which change fixes it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"then-the-model-then-serving\">Then the model, then serving\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Only when data and features match do you look at the model: evaluate the served version on a fresh labelled slice and compare with its gate result. Last, check serving: wrong model version loaded, stale cache, a fallback silently answering instead of the model.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4;n4&gt;n5;n5&gt;n6\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Debugging ML systems in production fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Start with what changed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Check the data, then the features\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replay the prediction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Then the model, then serving\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n5\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:25.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Make it stay fixed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n6\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">A bottom-up check from the shell\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Start with what changed to Check the data, then the features; 2, Check the data, then the features to Replay the prediction; 3, Replay the prediction to Then the model, then serving; 4, Then the model, then serving to Make it stay fixed; 5, Make it stay fixed to A bottom-up check from the shell.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Start with what changed \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> List what changed in the last week: data sources, feature code, model version, serving image, traffic mix.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not logging inputs, outputs and model version.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Check the data, then the features \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare today's inputs with the training set: null rates, ranges, new category values, row counts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fixing it without adding a check that would have caught it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Replay the prediction \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Log the model version, the input features and the output for a sample of requests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Then the model, then serving \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Only when data and features match do you look at the model: evaluate the served version on a fresh labelled slice and compare with its gate result.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retraining before checking the data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n5\">\u003cdt>Make it stay fixed \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add a data check at the pipeline boundary, a test that computes a feature the same way in training and serving, and an alert on the signal that would have caught this.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n6\">\u003cdt>A bottom-up check from the shell \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The data check finds the null rate, the comparison shows serving computes the feature differently, and the replay reproduces the bad output.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-before-you-look\">Predict before you look\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Say what you expect each check to show before running it. A wrong guess teaches you where your picture of the system is wrong.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Latency and errors are normal, but conversion from the recommendations has dropped for two days. What do you check first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> What changed in the last few days, then input and feature distributions against training, before touching the model.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A healthy service can still serve bad predictions, so start with data and features.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"make-it-stay-fixed\">Make it stay fixed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add a data check at the pipeline boundary, a test that computes a feature the same way in training and serving, and an alert on the signal that would have caught this. Write down the cause in one paragraph.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-bottom-up-check-from-the-shell\">A bottom-up check from the shell\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python check_inputs.py --window 48h --baseline training\npython compare_features.py --sample 500 --serving-vs-training\npython replay.py --request-id 7f3a --model churn-v14\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python check_inputs.py --window 48h --baseline training\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature days_since_login: 31% null (training: 0.2% null)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python compare_features.py --sample 500 --serving-vs-training\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature days_since_login: 29% of rows differ between serving and training\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python replay.py --request-id 7f3a --model churn-v14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">replayed output 0.12, logged output 0.12, expected 0.81\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The data check finds the null rate, the comparison shows serving computes the feature differently, and the replay reproduces the bad output. The model is fine. The output is a simulated illustration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-fails\">How it fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The team retrains the model first, which changes nothing because the fault is in a feature. Nobody logs inputs, so the bad prediction cannot be replayed. Several things are changed at once, so no one knows which fixed it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-retrain-that-fixed-nothing\">Incident: The retrain that fixed nothing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Conversion from recommendations fell for two days. The team retrained the model twice with no improvement. A feature pipeline had started filling a column with nulls after an upstream schema change.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python check_inputs.py --window 48h --baseline training\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python check_inputs.py --window 48h --baseline training\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature days_since_login: 31% null (training: 0.2% null)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The output is a simulated illustration. A data check at the pipeline boundary would have caught the nulls the day they began, before any retraining was tried.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interview-question\">Interview question\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;A model&#39;s results got worse in production. Where do you start?&quot; Check what changed, then data, then features against training, replay a bad prediction from logs, and only then the model and serving. Change one thing at a time and add a test afterwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Retraining before checking the data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not logging inputs, outputs and model version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several layers at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing it without adding a check that would have caught it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-what-changed","text":"Start with what changed"},{"id":"check-the-data-then-the-features","text":"Check the data, then the features"},{"id":"replay-the-prediction","text":"Replay the prediction"},{"id":"then-the-model-then-serving","text":"Then the model, then serving"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"predict-before-you-look","text":"Predict before you look"},{"id":"make-it-stay-fixed","text":"Make it stay fixed"},{"id":"a-bottom-up-check-from-the-shell","text":"A bottom-up check from the shell"},{"id":"how-it-fails","text":"How it fails"},{"id":"incident-the-retrain-that-fixed-nothing","text":"Incident: The retrain that fixed nothing"},{"id":"interview-question","text":"Interview question"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google: Rules of Machine Learning","u":"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/rules-of-ml"},{"t":"Evidently documentation","u":"https://docs.evidentlyai.com/"},{"t":"MLflow Tracking","u":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/tracking.html"}],"text":"When a model misbehaves, the model is rarely the first thing to blame. Work from the data upward and change one layer at a time, so that each check rules something out. What you will learn Walk a bottom-up checklist: data, features, model, serving Tell training-serving skew from real drift using logged inputs Replay a bad prediction from stored inputs and the model version Write the regression test that stops the same fault returning Start with what changed List what changed in the last week: data sources, feature code, model version, serving image, traffic mix. Most production faults follow a change. If nothing changed, suspect slow drift or an upstream system that changed without telling you. Check the data, then the features Compare today&#39;s inputs with the training set: null rates, ranges, new category values, row counts. Then compare the feature values the service computed with the values the training pipeline would compute for the same record. A difference is skew, and skew is a code fault, not a model fault. Replay the prediction Log the model version, the input features and the output for a sample of requests. With those three you can replay one bad prediction offline, get the same answer, and then change one thing at a time to see which change fixes it. Then the model, then serving Only when data and features match do you look at the model: evaluate the served version on a fresh labelled slice and compare with its gate result. Last, check serving: wrong model version loaded, stale cache, a fallback silently answering instead of the model. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Debugging ML systems in production fits together Flow in order: 1, Start with what changed to Check the data, then the features; 2, Check the data, then the features to Replay the prediction; 3, Replay the prediction to Then the model, then serving; 4, Then the model, then serving to Make it stay fixed; 5, Make it stay fixed to A bottom-up check from the shell. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Start with what changed Step What it does: List what changed in the last week: data sources, feature code, model version, serving image, traffic mix. If it fails or is weak: Not logging inputs, outputs and model version. Check the data, then the features Observability What it does: Compare today's inputs with the training set: null rates, ranges, new category values, row counts. If it fails or is weak: Fixing it without adding a check that would have caught it. Replay the prediction Step What it does: Log the model version, the input features and the output for a sample of requests. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Then the model, then serving Step What it does: Only when data and features match do you look at the model: evaluate the served version on a fresh labelled slice and compare with its gate result. If it fails or is weak: Retraining before checking the data. Make it stay fixed Step What it does: Add a data check at the pipeline boundary, a test that computes a feature the same way in training and serving, and an alert on the signal that would have caught this. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. A bottom-up check from the shell Observability What it does: The data check finds the null rate, the comparison shows serving computes the feature differently, and the replay reproduces the bad output. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Predict before you look Say what you expect each check to show before running it. A wrong guess teaches you where your picture of the system is wrong"},"mlops/feature-stores-and-data-versioning":{"html":"\u003cp>Most ML bugs are data bugs. If a feature means one thing in the training notebook and another in the serving code, the model learns something the service cannot reproduce. Shared definitions and versioned data remove that gap.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Training and serving skew and why it happens\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a feature store provides\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Point-in-time correctness and leakage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning datasets and lineage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"skew\">Skew\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Training reads history from a warehouse with batch code. Serving computes the same value from live data with different code. Small differences, such as null handling, time zones or rounding, cause a model to behave worse than any offline test suggested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-definition\">One definition\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# a feature defined once, used for offline and online\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom feast import Entity, FeatureView, Field, FileSource\nfrom feast.types import Float32, Int64\n\ncustomer = Entity(name=&quot;customer_id&quot;, join_keys=[&quot;customer_id&quot;])\norders_stats = FeatureView(\n    name=&quot;orders_stats&quot;,\n    entities=[customer],\n    ttl=timedelta(days=2),\n    schema=[Field(name=&quot;orders_7d&quot;, dtype=Int64), Field(name=&quot;avg_basket&quot;, dtype=Float32)],\n    source=FileSource(path=&quot;data/orders_stats.parquet&quot;, timestamp_field=&quot;event_ts&quot;),\n)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># a feature defined once, used for offline and online\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> datetime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> timedelta\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> feast \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> Entity, FeatureView, Field, FileSource\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> feast.types \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> Float32, Int64\n\ncustomer = Entity(name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer_id&quot;\u003c/span>, join_keys=[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;customer_id&quot;\u003c/span>])\norders_stats = FeatureView(\n    name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orders_stats&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    entities=[customer],\n    ttl=timedelta(days=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>),\n    schema=[Field(name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orders_7d&quot;\u003c/span>, dtype=Int64), Field(name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;avg_basket&quot;\u003c/span>, dtype=Float32)],\n    source=FileSource(path=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;data/orders_stats.parquet&quot;\u003c/span>, timestamp_field=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;event_ts&quot;\u003c/span>),\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>offline store\u003c/strong> holds history for training, and the \u003cstrong>online store\u003c/strong> serves the latest values with low latency. A materialization job keeps them in sync.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"point-in-time\">Point in time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When building training rows, use only data that was available \u003cstrong>at the time of each label\u003c/strong>. Using a later value leaks the future into the model and inflates results. Feature stores provide point-in-time joins for this.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"version-everything\">Version everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record the dataset version, feature definitions, code commit and parameters with every trained model. Tools such as DVC, lakehouse table versions or MLflow datasets let you reload exactly what a model saw.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dvc add data/train.parquet\ngit add data/train.parquet.dvc &amp;&amp; git commit -m &quot;train data v12&quot;\nmlflow runs describe --run-id &quot;$RUN_ID&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">dvc add data/train.parquet\ngit add data/train.parquet.dvc &amp;&amp; git commit -m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;train data v12&quot;\u003c/span>\nmlflow runs describe --run-id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$RUN_ID\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Monitor \u003cstrong>freshness\u003c/strong> of online features. Stale features silently degrade predictions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Feature stores and data versioning fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Skew\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">One definition\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Point in time\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Version everything\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Skew to One definition; 2, One definition to Point in time; 3, Point in time to Version everything.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Skew \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Training reads history from a warehouse with batch code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Separate code for training and serving features.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>One definition \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The offline store holds history for training, and the online store serves the latest values with low latency.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using future data in training rows.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Point in time \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> When building training rows, use only data that was available at the time of each label.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Version everything \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Record the dataset version, feature definitions, code commit and parameters with every trained model.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring stale online features.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-perfect-offline-poor-online\">Incident: Perfect offline, poor online\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model&#39;s accuracy dropped after launch because of a feature mismatch.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">offline AUC: 0.91\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">online AUC first week: 0.74\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">feature avg_basket differs from offline value in 38% of requests\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The service computed an average with a different window than the training job. Comparing logged online features with the offline values revealed it. The team moved both to one feature definition and added a daily skew check.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Separate code for training and serving features\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using future data in training rows\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unversioned datasets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring stale online features\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"skew","text":"Skew"},{"id":"one-definition","text":"One definition"},{"id":"point-in-time","text":"Point in time"},{"id":"version-everything","text":"Version everything"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-perfect-offline-poor-online","text":"Incident: Perfect offline, poor online"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"MLflow documentation","u":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/index.html"},{"t":"Kubeflow documentation","u":"https://www.kubeflow.org/docs/"}],"text":"Most ML bugs are data bugs. If a feature means one thing in the training notebook and another in the serving code, the model learns something the service cannot reproduce. Shared definitions and versioned data remove that gap. What you will learn Training and serving skew and why it happens What a feature store provides Point-in-time correctness and leakage Versioning datasets and lineage Skew Training reads history from a warehouse with batch code. Serving computes the same value from live data with different code. Small differences, such as null handling, time zones or rounding, cause a model to behave worse than any offline test suggested. One definition # a feature defined once, used for offline and online from datetime import timedelta from feast import Entity, FeatureView, Field, FileSource from feast.types import Float32, Int64 customer = Entity(name= \"customer_id\" , join_keys=[ \"customer_id\" ]) orders_stats = FeatureView( name= \"orders_stats\" , entities=[customer], ttl=timedelta(days= 2 ), schema=[Field(name= \"orders_7d\" , dtype=Int64), Field(name= \"avg_basket\" , dtype=Float32)], source=FileSource(path= \"data/orders_stats.parquet\" , timestamp_field= \"event_ts\" ), ) The offline store holds history for training, and the online store serves the latest values with low latency. A materialization job keeps them in sync. Point in time When building training rows, use only data that was available at the time of each label . Using a later value leaks the future into the model and inflates results. Feature stores provide point-in-time joins for this. Version everything Record the dataset version, feature definitions, code commit and parameters with every trained model. Tools such as DVC, lakehouse table versions or MLflow datasets let you reload exactly what a model saw. dvc add data/train.parquet git add data/train.parquet.dvc && git commit -m \"train data v12\" mlflow runs describe --run-id \" $RUN_ID \" Monitor freshness of online features. Stale features silently degrade predictions. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Feature stores and data versioning fits together Flow in order: 1, Skew to One definition; 2, One definition to Point in time; 3, Point in time to Version everything. Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Skew Step What it does: Training reads history from a warehouse with batch code. If it fails or is weak: Separate code for training and serving features. One definition Step What it does: The offline store holds history for training, and the online store serves the latest values with low latency. If it fails or is weak: Using future data in training rows. Point in time Step What it does: When building training rows, use only data that was available at the time of each label. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Version everything Step What it does: Record the dataset version, feature definitions, code commit and parameters with every trained model. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring stale online features. Incident: Perfect offline, poor online Incident A model&#39;s accuracy dropped after launch because of a feature mismatch. offline AUC: 0.91 online AUC first week: 0.74 feature avg_basket differs from offline value in 38% of requests The service computed an average with a different window than the training job. Comparing logged online features with the offline values revealed it. The team moved both to one feature definition and added a daily skew check. Common mistakes Separate code for training and serving features Using future data in training rows Unversioned datasets Ignoring stale online features"},"mlops/model-evaluation-ab-tests-and-shadow-deployments":{"html":"\u003cp>Shipping a model is a change to a live system, so it deserves the same discipline as any release. The extra difficulty is that quality is statistical and can differ between groups of users.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing metrics that match the goal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Slice analysis and fairness checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shadow deployments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A/B tests, guardrails and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metrics-and-slices\">Metrics and slices\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick a primary metric tied to the business goal and add guardrails such as latency, cost and error rate. Overall averages hide problems, so compute metrics by \u003cstrong>slice\u003c/strong>: new versus returning users, region, device, language and rare classes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_parquet(&quot;eval_predictions.parquet&quot;)\nby_slice = df.groupby(&quot;segment&quot;).apply(lambda g: (g.pred == g.label).mean())\nprint(by_slice.sort_values().head())\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> pandas \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">as\u003c/span> pd\ndf = pd.read_parquet(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;eval_predictions.parquet&quot;\u003c/span>)\nby_slice = df.groupby(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;segment&quot;\u003c/span>).apply(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda\u003c/span> g: (g.pred == g.label).mean())\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(by_slice.sort_values().head())\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shadow-deployment\">Shadow deployment\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In \u003cstrong>shadow mode\u003c/strong> the new model receives a copy of live requests, but its output is not used. Compare outputs, latency and errors with the current model. This catches crashes, performance problems and surprising predictions without risking users.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"online-experiments\">Online experiments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"experiment: ranker-v7\nsplit: {control: 90, treatment: 10}\nprimary_metric: purchase_rate\nguardrails: {p95_latency_ms: &quot;&lt;= 250&quot;, error_rate: &quot;&lt;= 0.5%&quot;}\nmin_duration_days: 14\nstop_rules: {guardrail_breach: abort}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">experiment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ranker-v7\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">split:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">control:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">treatment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">primary_metric:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">purchase_rate\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">guardrails:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">p95_latency_ms:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&lt;= 250&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">error_rate:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&lt;= 0.5%&quot;\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">min_duration_days:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stop_rules:\u003c/span> {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">guardrail_breach:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">abort\u003c/span>}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Decide the duration and sample size before starting, so you do not stop when the numbers look good. Random assignment must be stable per user. Watch guardrails continuously and abort on a breach. After a win, ramp up gradually and keep the old model available for quick rollback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"record\">Record\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store the evaluation report with the model version, data version and decision. This helps future reviews and audits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Model evaluation, A/B tests and shadow deployments fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Metrics and slices\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shadow deployment\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Online experiments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Metrics and slices to Shadow deployment; 2, Shadow deployment to Online experiments; 3, Online experiments to Record.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Metrics and slices \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pick a primary metric tied to the business goal and add guardrails such as latency, cost and error rate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Judging only by overall averages.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Shadow deployment \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> In shadow mode the new model receives a copy of live requests, but its output is not used.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> This catches crashes, performance problems and surprising predictions without risking users.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Online experiments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Decide the duration and sample size before starting, so you do not stop when the numbers look good.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stopping an experiment when it first looks good.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Record \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Store the evaluation report with the model version, data version and decision.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-better-on-average-worse-for-new-users\">Incident: Better on average, worse for new users\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model improved overall metrics but hurt a user segment.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">overall conversion: +1.8%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">new users: -6.2%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">returning users: +3.1%\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The model relied on purchase history that new users did not have. The overall average hid the drop because new users were a minority. The team added a slice report to every evaluation and a fallback model for users with no history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Judging only by overall averages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stopping an experiment when it first looks good\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No guardrail metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No way to roll back quickly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"metrics-and-slices","text":"Metrics and slices"},{"id":"shadow-deployment","text":"Shadow deployment"},{"id":"online-experiments","text":"Online experiments"},{"id":"record","text":"Record"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-better-on-average-worse-for-new-users","text":"Incident: Better on average, worse for new users"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"MLflow documentation","u":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/index.html"},{"t":"Evidently AI documentation","u":"https://docs.evidentlyai.com/"}],"text":"Shipping a model is a change to a live system, so it deserves the same discipline as any release. The extra difficulty is that quality is statistical and can differ between groups of users. What you will learn Choosing metrics that match the goal Slice analysis and fairness checks Shadow deployments A/B tests, guardrails and rollback Metrics and slices Pick a primary metric tied to the business goal and add guardrails such as latency, cost and error rate. Overall averages hide problems, so compute metrics by slice : new versus returning users, region, device, language and rare classes. import pandas as pd df = pd.read_parquet( \"eval_predictions.parquet\" ) by_slice = df.groupby( \"segment\" ).apply( lambda g: (g.pred == g.label).mean()) print (by_slice.sort_values().head()) Shadow deployment In shadow mode the new model receives a copy of live requests, but its output is not used. Compare outputs, latency and errors with the current model. This catches crashes, performance problems and surprising predictions without risking users. Online experiments experiment: ranker-v7 split: { control: 90 , treatment: 10 } primary_metric: purchase_rate guardrails: { p95_latency_ms: \"\u003c= 250\" , error_rate: \"\u003c= 0.5%\" } min_duration_days: 14 stop_rules: { guardrail_breach: abort } Decide the duration and sample size before starting, so you do not stop when the numbers look good. Random assignment must be stable per user. Watch guardrails continuously and abort on a breach. After a win, ramp up gradually and keep the old model available for quick rollback. Record Store the evaluation report with the model version, data version and decision. This helps future reviews and audits. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How Model evaluation, A/B tests and shadow deployments fits together Flow in order: 1, Metrics and slices to Shadow deployment; 2, Shadow deployment to Online experiments; 3, Online experiments to Record. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Metrics and slices Observability What it does: Pick a primary metric tied to the business goal and add guardrails such as latency, cost and error rate. If it fails or is weak: Judging only by overall averages. Shadow deployment Step What it does: In shadow mode the new model receives a copy of live requests, but its output is not used. If it fails or is weak: This catches crashes, performance problems and surprising predictions without risking users. Online experiments Step What it does: Decide the duration and sample size before starting, so you do not stop when the numbers look good. If it fails or is weak: Stopping an experiment when it first looks good. Record Step What it does: Store the evaluation report with the model version, data version and decision. If it fails or is weak: Check the Common mistakes list below this diagram for what usually goes wrong here. Incident: Better on average, worse for new users Incident A model improved overall metrics but hurt a user segment. overall conversion: +1.8% new users: -6.2% returning users: +3.1% The model relied on purchase history that new users did not have. The overall average hid the drop because new users were a minority. The team added a slice report to every evaluation and a fallback model for users with no history. Common mistakes Judging only by overall averages Stopping an experiment when it first looks good No guardrail metrics No way to roll back quickly"},"mlops/ml-pipelines-and-workflow-orchestration":{"html":"\u003cp>A pipeline makes every step explicit: where data comes from, how it is checked, how the model is trained and what must be true before it is released. That is the foundation for continuous training.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Breaking work into pipeline steps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing data and artifacts between steps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retries, caching and resource requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scheduling and triggering retraining\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"steps\">Steps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A typical pipeline: \u003cstrong>ingest\u003c/strong> data, \u003cstrong>validate\u003c/strong> it, \u003cstrong>build features\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>train\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>evaluate\u003c/strong> against a threshold, \u003cstrong>register\u003c/strong> the model and optionally \u003cstrong>deploy\u003c/strong>. Each step is a container or function with clear inputs and outputs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from airflow.decorators import dag, task\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\n@dag(schedule=&quot;0 3 * * 1&quot;, start_date=datetime(2026, 1, 1), catchup=False)\ndef retrain_churn():\n    @task(retries=2)\n    def validate(ds=None):\n        return run_data_checks(ds)           # returns a dataset version\n\n    @task\n    def train(dataset_version):\n        return train_model(dataset_version)  # returns a model run id\n\n    @task\n    def evaluate(run_id):\n        score = evaluate_model(run_id)\n        if score &lt; 0.82:\n            raise ValueError(&quot;model below threshold&quot;)\n        return run_id\n\n    @task\n    def register(run_id):\n        register_model(run_id, stage=&quot;staging&quot;)\n\n    register(evaluate(train(validate())))\n\nretrain_churn()\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> airflow.decorators \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> dag, task\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> datetime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> datetime\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">@dag(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">schedule=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0 3 * * 1&quot;\u003c/span>, start_date=datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\u003c/span>), catchup=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">retrain_churn\u003c/span>():\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">    @task(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">retries=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">validate\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">ds=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>\u003c/span>):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> run_data_checks(ds)           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># returns a dataset version\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">    @task\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">train\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">dataset_version\u003c/span>):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> train_model(dataset_version)  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># returns a model run id\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">    @task\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">evaluate\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">run_id\u003c/span>):\n        score = evaluate_model(run_id)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> score &lt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.82\u003c/span>:\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> ValueError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;model below threshold&quot;\u003c/span>)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> run_id\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">    @task\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">register\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">run_id\u003c/span>):\n        register_model(run_id, stage=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;staging&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\n    register(evaluate(train(validate())))\n\nretrain_churn()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-between-steps\">Data between steps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pass \u003cstrong>references\u003c/strong>, such as dataset versions and object storage paths, not large data through the orchestrator. Store artifacts in object storage or an artifact store with versioning.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reliability\">Reliability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set retries with backoff for transient failures, but not for logic errors. Cache steps when inputs and code have not changed. Set resource requests, for example GPUs for training only. Make each step \u003cstrong>idempotent\u003c/strong>, so rerunning does not duplicate or corrupt results.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"triggers\">Triggers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run on a schedule, when new data arrives or when drift monitoring requests it. Require a successful evaluation gate before anything is registered, and send alerts on failed runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How ML pipelines and workflow orchestration fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Steps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Data between steps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reliability\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Triggers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Steps to Data between steps; 2, Data between steps to Reliability; 3, Reliability to Triggers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Steps \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A typical pipeline: ingest data, validate it, build features, train, evaluate against a threshold, register the model and optionally deploy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Passing large data through the orchestrator.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Data between steps \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pass references, such as dataset versions and object storage paths, not large data through the orchestrator.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Steps that are not idempotent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Reliability \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Set retries with backoff for transient failures, but not for logic errors.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Notebooks as the only process.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Triggers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run on a schedule, when new data arrives or when drift monitoring requests it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No evaluation gate before registration.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-notebook-that-nobody-could-rerun\">Incident: The notebook that nobody could rerun\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model could not be retrained because the process lived in a notebook.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FileNotFoundError: /Users/amir/data/clean_v3.csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">notebook cell 14 depends on cell 31 being run first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">retraining blocked for 3 weeks\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The author had left, and the notebook referenced a local file and an undocumented order of cells. The team rebuilt the process as a pipeline with versioned data, explicit steps and tests, and retrained in an afternoon.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Notebooks as the only process\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing large data through the orchestrator\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Steps that are not idempotent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No evaluation gate before registration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps"},{"id":"data-between-steps","text":"Data between steps"},{"id":"reliability","text":"Reliability"},{"id":"triggers","text":"Triggers"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-notebook-that-nobody-could-rerun","text":"Incident: The notebook that nobody could rerun"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubeflow Pipelines","u":"https://www.kubeflow.org/docs/components/pipelines/"},{"t":"Apache Airflow documentation","u":"https://airflow.apache.org/docs/"}],"text":"A pipeline makes every step explicit: where data comes from, how it is checked, how the model is trained and what must be true before it is released. That is the foundation for continuous training. What you will learn Breaking work into pipeline steps Passing data and artifacts between steps Retries, caching and resource requests Scheduling and triggering retraining Steps A typical pipeline: ingest data, validate it, build features , train , evaluate against a threshold, register the model and optionally deploy . Each step is a container or function with clear inputs and outputs. from airflow.decorators import dag, task from datetime import datetime @dag( schedule= \"0 3 * * 1\" , start_date=datetime( 2026 , 1 , 1 ), catchup= False ) def retrain_churn (): @task( retries= 2 ) def validate ( ds= None ): return run_data_checks(ds) # returns a dataset version @task def train ( dataset_version ): return train_model(dataset_version) # returns a model run id @task def evaluate ( run_id ): score = evaluate_model(run_id) if score \u003c 0.82 : raise ValueError( \"model below threshold\" ) return run_id @task def register ( run_id ): register_model(run_id, stage= \"staging\" ) register(evaluate(train(validate()))) retrain_churn() Data between steps Pass references , such as dataset versions and object storage paths, not large data through the orchestrator. Store artifacts in object storage or an artifact store with versioning. Reliability Set retries with backoff for transient failures, but not for logic errors. Cache steps when inputs and code have not changed. Set resource requests, for example GPUs for training only. Make each step idempotent , so rerunning does not duplicate or corrupt results. Triggers Run on a schedule, when new data arrives or when drift monitoring requests it. Require a successful evaluation gate before anything is registered, and send alerts on failed runs. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How ML pipelines and workflow orchestration fits together Flow in order: 1, Steps to Data between steps; 2, Data between steps to Reliability; 3, Reliability to Triggers. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Steps Step What it does: A typical pipeline: ingest data, validate it, build features, train, evaluate against a threshold, register the model and optionally deploy. If it fails or is weak: Passing large data through the orchestrator. Data between steps Data store What it does: Pass references, such as dataset versions and object storage paths, not large data through the orchestrator. If it fails or is weak: Steps that are not idempotent. Reliability Step What it does: Set retries with backoff for transient failures, but not for logic errors. If it fails or is weak: Notebooks as the only process. Triggers Step What it does: Run on a schedule, when new data arrives or when drift monitoring requests it. If it fails or is weak: No evaluation gate before registration. Incident: The notebook that nobody could rerun Incident A model could not be retrained because the process lived in a notebook. FileNotFoundError: /Users/amir/data/clean_v3.csv notebook cell 14 depends on cell 31 being run first retraining blocked for 3 weeks The author had left, and the notebook referenced a local file and an undocumented order of cells. The team rebuilt the process as a pipeline with versioned data, explicit steps and tests, and retrained in an afternoon. Common mistakes Notebooks as the only process Passing large data through the orchestrator Steps that are not idempotent No evaluation gate before registration"},"mlops/llmops-prompts-evals-and-versioning":{"html":"\u003cp>Classic MLOps manages models you train. LLMOps manages applications built on models you call, where behavior is changed mostly through prompts, retrieval and model selection. The discipline is the same: version, test, release, observe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prompts as versioned artifacts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning models and planning upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Evaluation suites in CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitoring cost, latency and quality\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prompts-in-version-control\">Prompts in version control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# prompts/ticket_summary/v3.yaml\nname: ticket_summary\nversion: 3\nmodel: claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929\ntemperature: 0.2\nsystem: |\n  You summarize support tickets for engineers. Be factual and brief.\ntemplate: |\n  Ticket:\n  {{ticket_text}}\n  Write a summary of at most 3 sentences and list the product area.\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># prompts/ticket_summary/v3.yaml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ticket_summary\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">model:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">temperature:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">system:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n  You summarize support tickets for engineers. Be factual and brief.\n\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n  Ticket:\n  {{ticket_text}}\n  Write a summary of at most 3 sentences and list the product area.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Store prompts with their model settings, review changes in pull requests and tag releases. Avoid editing prompts only in a vendor console where history is lost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pin-and-upgrade\">Pin and upgrade\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use specific model versions instead of moving aliases. When a new model appears, run the evaluation suite, compare quality and cost and roll out gradually.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"evaluate-in-ci\">Evaluate in CI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python evals/run.py --prompt prompts/ticket_summary/v3.yaml --cases evals/tickets.jsonl --out results.json\npython evals/check.py results.json --min-score 0.85 --max-cost-per-1k 4.00\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">python evals/run.py --prompt prompts/ticket_summary/v3.yaml --cases evals/tickets.jsonl --out results.json\npython evals/check.py results.json --min-score 0.85 --max-cost-per-1k 4.00\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cases come from real examples. Combine exact checks (valid JSON, required fields), rule checks (no personal data) and graded checks for quality. Fail the pipeline on regression.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"observe\">Observe\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record per request: prompt version, model version, input and output tokens, latency, cost, tool calls and user feedback, with sensitive data redacted. Alert on cost per request, error rate and format failures. Add failing real cases to the evaluation set, so the suite improves with experience.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-parts-fit-together\">How the parts fit together\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"n1&gt;n2;n2&gt;n3;n3&gt;n4\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How LLMOps: versioning prompts, models and evaluations fits together\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Prompts in version control\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pin and upgrade\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n3\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Evaluate in CI\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"n4\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Observe\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Prompts in version control to Pin and upgrade; 2, Pin and upgrade to Evaluate in CI; 3, Evaluate in CI to Observe.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n1\">\u003cdt>Prompts in version control \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Store prompts with their model settings, review changes in pull requests and tag releases.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Prompts edited in consoles without history.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n2\">\u003cdt>Pin and upgrade \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use specific model versions instead of moving aliases.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Using moving model aliases in production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n3\">\u003cdt>Evaluate in CI \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cases come from real examples. Combine exact checks (valid JSON, required fields), rule checks (no personal data) and graded checks for quality.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not tracking cost per request.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"n4\">\u003cdt>Observe \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Record per request: prompt version, model version, input and output tokens, latency, cost, tool calls and user feedback, with sensitive data redacted.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No evaluation set.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-alias-that-changed\">Incident: The alias that changed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A model alias moved to a new version and broke JSON parsing.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">model: provider-latest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">output now starts with &quot;Sure, here is the JSON:&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">parser error rate: 0% to 17%\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team used an alias for convenience, and the provider updated it. The new version added a polite preface before the JSON. They pinned an exact model version, added a format check to evaluations and scheduled upgrades with testing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using moving model aliases in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prompts edited in consoles without history\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No evaluation set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not tracking cost per request\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"prompts-in-version-control","text":"Prompts in version control"},{"id":"pin-and-upgrade","text":"Pin and upgrade"},{"id":"evaluate-in-ci","text":"Evaluate in CI"},{"id":"observe","text":"Observe"},{"id":"how-the-parts-fit-together","text":"How the parts fit together"},{"id":"incident-the-alias-that-changed","text":"Incident: The alias that changed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Anthropic: develop test cases","u":"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"},{"t":"MLflow documentation","u":"https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/index.html"}],"text":"Classic MLOps manages models you train. LLMOps manages applications built on models you call, where behavior is changed mostly through prompts, retrieval and model selection. The discipline is the same: version, test, release, observe. What you will learn Prompts as versioned artifacts Pinning models and planning upgrades Evaluation suites in CI Monitoring cost, latency and quality Prompts in version control # prompts/ticket_summary/v3.yaml name: ticket_summary version: 3 model: claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929 temperature: 0.2 system: | You summarize support tickets for engineers. Be factual and brief. template: | Ticket: {{ticket_text}} Write a summary of at most 3 sentences and list the product area. Store prompts with their model settings, review changes in pull requests and tag releases. Avoid editing prompts only in a vendor console where history is lost. Pin and upgrade Use specific model versions instead of moving aliases. When a new model appears, run the evaluation suite, compare quality and cost and roll out gradually. Evaluate in CI python evals/run.py --prompt prompts/ticket_summary/v3.yaml --cases evals/tickets.jsonl --out results.json python evals/check.py results.json --min-score 0.85 --max-cost-per-1k 4.00 Cases come from real examples. Combine exact checks (valid JSON, required fields), rule checks (no personal data) and graded checks for quality. Fail the pipeline on regression. Observe Record per request: prompt version, model version, input and output tokens, latency, cost, tool calls and user feedback, with sensitive data redacted. Alert on cost per request, error rate and format failures. Add failing real cases to the evaluation set, so the suite improves with experience. How the parts fit together The lesson in one picture: each box is a section of this lesson, in the order you meet it. Select a box to see what it does and the mistake that usually goes with it. How LLMOps: versioning prompts, models and evaluations fits together Flow in order: 1, Prompts in version control to Pin and upgrade; 2, Pin and upgrade to Evaluate in CI; 3, Evaluate in CI to Observe. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Prompts in version control Step What it does: Store prompts with their model settings, review changes in pull requests and tag releases. If it fails or is weak: Prompts edited in consoles without history. Pin and upgrade Step What it does: Use specific model versions instead of moving aliases. If it fails or is weak: Using moving model aliases in production. Evaluate in CI Step What it does: Cases come from real examples. Combine exact checks (valid JSON, required fields), rule checks (no personal data) and graded checks for quality. If it fails or is weak: Not tracking cost per request. Observe Observability What it does: Record per request: prompt version, model version, input and output tokens, latency, cost, tool calls and user feedback, with sensitive data redacted. If it fails or is weak: No evaluation set. Incident: The alias that changed Incident A model alias moved to a new version and broke JSON parsing. model: provider-latest output now starts with \"Sure, here is the JSON:\" parser error rate: 0% to 17% The team used an alias for convenience, and the provider updated it. The new version added a polite preface before the JSON. They pinned an exact model version, added a format check to evaluations and scheduled upgrades with testing. Common mistakes Using moving model aliases in production Prompts edited in consoles without history No evaluation set Not tracking cost per request"}});